IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `lrnt-dates`

-- Law Reform (Miscellaneous Provisions) Act 1956 (NT), Parts IV to X -- the claims machinery around Part II.
--
-- SEAT: the individual each provision is applied to. Mostly the person bringing a claim: the injured person, a
-- dependant or other person who suffers damage through the injured person (s 16, s 17(2), s 18), a person who sues more
-- than one wrongdoer (s 12(3)), a worker hurt by a fellow worker (s 22), a family member who suffers nervous shock
-- (s 25), a person with a claim against an insured wrongdoer (Part VIII), a property owner whose property a child damaged
-- (s 29A), a person hurt by a dog (s 32), a blind or deaf person with a guide dog or hearing dog (s 35). Where the Act
-- addresses another individual, that individual: the employee who committed a tort (s 22A), the person who killed or
-- injured a dog (ss 33, 34). The court and the Territory get no seat: what a court would find is asked as a fact.
--
-- Every question takes plain facts. No question takes a DEONTIC or an EVENT. The Parts have no duty with a deadline
-- that the claimant meets (s 25(5) fixes a court, not a time).
--
-- PROVISIONS COVERED: s 12(2), (3) and s 11(b) (several actions for the same damage); s 15, s 16, s 17(2), s 18, s 21,
-- s 21A (contributory negligence); s 22 (common employment abolished); s 22A (vicarious liability: indemnity); s 23,
-- s 24, s 25 (mental or nervous shock); s 26, s 27, s 28 (charge on insurance moneys); s 29A (property damage by
-- children); s 32 (dogs), s 33, s 34 (killing or injuring a dog), s 35 (guide dogs and hearing dogs). OUT, with reasons
-- in NOTES.md: s 12(4), (5), s 13, s 14 and s 19 (contribution between wrongdoers), s 17(1), s 20 (procedure), s 26(3)
-- (ranking among claimants), s 29, s 9(2) (savings), ss 1, 3, 4 (machinery).
--
-- Edition: Law Reform (Miscellaneous Provisions) Act 1956 (NT), reprint REPL004 as in force at 6 December 2018,
-- deposited at ../../registers/source-bundle/Law-Reform-Miscellaneous-Provisions-Act-1956-NT-current-reprint-REPL004.txt.
--
-- Copyright in Northern Territory legislation is owned by the Northern Territory. Quoted from an unofficial copy under
-- the NT copyright policy of 8 October 1996 and the owner's ruling of 24 October 2026 (SOURCE-LICENSE.md).
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Not for public use as advice. Answers follow the rulings on negatives of 2026-11-04 and 2026-11-05
-- (NOTES.md): "(the text's no)" marks the text's own no; "NOTHING-kind" marks a limit in the text's own wording beyond
-- which it says nothing.

§ `Parts IV to X -- the questions of the person bringing or answering a claim`

-- The Ordinance commenced on 28 June 1956 (endnotes; s 21(b)). Part VIIIA (s 29A) was inserted by Act No. 44 of 1991,
-- commenced 1 January 1992 (Gaz S63, 16 December 1991). The Law Reform (Miscellaneous Provisions) Amendment Act 2001
-- (Act No. 12, 2001), which replaced s 16 and inserted s 21A, commenced on 1 August 2001 (Gaz G29, 25 July 2001, p 2).
`the commencement of the Ordinance` MEANS `the calendar date` 28 6 1956
`the commencement of s 29A` MEANS `the calendar date` 1 1 1992
`the commencement of the 2001 amendments` MEANS `the calendar date` 1 8 2001

-- "after the commencement": an event on the commencement day counts as after it (fork F1).
GIVEN `the day` IS A DATE, `the commencement` IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the day` `is on or after` `the commencement` IF
    NOT (`the day` `falls before` `the commencement`)

§§ `s 12(2), (3) -- Suing more than one wrongdoer for the same damage`

-- s 12(2): judgment against one tort-feasor is not a bar to an action against another who would be liable as a joint
-- tort-feasor for the same damage.
-- s 12(3): if more than one action is brought for the damage, by or for the person who suffered it (or the estate, or a
-- spouse, de facto partner, sibling, half-sibling, parent or child), against tort-feasors liable for it:
--   (a) the sums recoverable under the judgments do not in the aggregate exceed the damages awarded by the judgment
--       first given; and
--   (b) in any action other than the one in which judgment is first given, the plaintiff is not entitled to costs unless
--       the court is of opinion that there was reasonable ground for bringing the action.
-- s 11(b): "the judgment first given" is, if reversed on appeal, the first judgment not so reversed, and, if varied, that
-- judgment as varied (the @desc asks for it so).

@export If I bring more than one action against different wrongdoers for the same damage, what is the most I can recover in total (Law Reform (Miscellaneous Provisions) Act 1956 (NT) s 12(3)(a))? The total recoverable under all the judgments cannot exceed the damages awarded by the judgment given first.
GIVEN `first judgment damages` IS A NUMBER @desc The damages (in dollars) awarded by the judgment given first (if it was reversed on appeal, the first judgment not reversed; if varied, as varied)
      `other judgments damages` IS A NUMBER @desc The damages (in dollars) awarded by all the other judgments together (0 if there is only one action)
GIVETH A NUMBER
`s 12(3)(a) -- the most I can recover in total from several actions` `first judgment damages` `other judgments damages` MEANS
    IF (`first judgment damages` PLUS `other judgments damages`) GREATER THAN `first judgment damages`
    THEN `first judgment damages`
    ELSE `first judgment damages` PLUS `other judgments damages`

DECLARE `Do I get my costs in this action` IS ONE OF
    `s 12(3)(b) does not limit your costs: judgment was first given in this action`
    `you may get costs: the court is of opinion there was reasonable ground for bringing this action (s 12(3)(b))`
    `no costs: this is not the action in which judgment was first given, and the court is not of opinion there was reasonable ground for bringing it (s 12(3)(b), the text's no)`
    `s 12(3)(b) does not apply: only one action has been brought for the damage (the text's no: its "if" fails)`

@export Can I get my costs in a second or later action against another wrongdoer for the same damage (Law Reform (Miscellaneous Provisions) Act 1956 (NT) s 12(3)(b))? Not unless the court thinks there was reasonable ground for bringing the action.
GIVEN `more than one action` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether more than one action has been brought for the damage against wrongdoers liable for it
      `judgment first given here` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether this action is the one in which judgment was first given
      `reasonable ground` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court is of opinion that there was reasonable ground for bringing this action
GIVETH A `Do I get my costs in this action`
`s 12(3)(b) -- do I get my costs in this action` `more than one action` `judgment first given here` `reasonable ground` MEANS
    IF NOT `more than one action`
    THEN `s 12(3)(b) does not apply: only one action has been brought for the damage (the text's no: its "if" fails)`
    ELSE IF `judgment first given here`
         THEN `s 12(3)(b) does not limit your costs: judgment was first given in this action`
         ELSE IF `reasonable ground`
              THEN `you may get costs: the court is of opinion there was reasonable ground for bringing this action (s 12(3)(b))`
              ELSE `no costs: this is not the action in which judgment was first given, and the court is not of opinion there was reasonable ground for bringing it (s 12(3)(b), the text's no)`

§§ `Part V -- Contributory negligence (s 15, s 16, s 17(2), s 18, s 21, s 21A)`

-- s 15(1): "wrong means an act or omission that: (a) gives rise to a liability in the tort of negligence; (b) amounts to
-- a breach of a contractual duty of care that is concurrent with a duty of care in tort; or (c) amounts to a breach of
-- statutory duty." A "means" definition. s 15(2): a person's wrong includes one they are vicariously responsible for
-- (asked as part of the fact). "damage includes loss of life and personal injury."
DECLARE `The other person's act` IS ONE OF
    `negligence (a tort of negligence)`
    `a breach of a contractual duty of care that runs alongside a duty of care in tort`
    `a breach of statutory duty`
    `something else (for example an intentional tort, or a breach of contract with no duty of care in tort)`

GIVEN `the act` IS A `The other person's act`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a wrong within s 15` `the act` IF
    NOT (`the act` EQUALS `something else (for example an intentional tort, or a breach of contract with no duty of care in tort)`)

-- Who is claiming: the person who suffered the damage (s 16), a dependant under the Compensation (Fatal Injuries) Act
-- 1974 of a person who died partly through their own wrong (s 17(2): reduced "to a proportionate extent"), or a third
-- person who suffers damage because of the injured person's damage (s 18: the injured person's wrong counts as the third
-- person's). In each case the reduction is the one made for the injured person's share.
DECLARE `Who is claiming` IS ONE OF
    `I suffered the damage myself`
    `I am a dependant claiming under the Compensation (Fatal Injuries) Act 1974 for the death of a person who was partly at fault`
    `I suffered damage because of the injured person's damage (for example loss of their society or services)`

-- s 21: s 16 does not apply to (a) a claim to which s 259 of the Navigation Act 1912 (Cth) applies, or (b) a case where
-- the wrong occurred before 28 June 1956. A flat negative: the text's no.
-- s 21A(3): the s 16 in force before 1 August 2001 continues to apply to a wrong committed before that day if
-- proceedings were commenced, or the persons responsible had agreed to settle, before that day. That earlier s 16 is
-- not deposited: the answer says so (NOTHING-kind). s 21A(2): otherwise the current s 16 applies to earlier wrongs too.
-- s 16(1): "If a person suffers damage as the result partly of the person's failure to take reasonable care and partly
-- of the wrong of another person": the "if" failing is the text's no.
-- s 16(2): s 16(1) "does not operate to defeat any defence arising under a contract": a flat negative.
DECLARE `Does apportionment for contributory negligence apply to my claim` IS ONE OF
    `yes: your claim is not defeated by your own share of fault, but your damages are reduced as the court thinks just and equitable (s 16(1))`
    `no: s 16 does not apply to a claim under s 259 of the Navigation Act 1912 (Cth) (s 21(a), the text's no)`
    `no: s 16 does not apply where the wrong occurred before 28 June 1956 (s 21(b), the text's no)`
    `the earlier s 16 applies: the wrong was before 1 August 2001 and proceedings were commenced, or a settlement agreed, before that day (s 21A(3)); that earlier text is not encoded here (NOTHING-kind)`
    `s 16 does not apply: the damage was not partly the result of your own failure to take reasonable care and partly of another person's wrong (negligence, a concurrent contractual duty of care, or breach of statutory duty) (the text's no: its "if" fails)`
    `s 16 does not save the claim from a defence arising under a contract (s 16(2), the text's no)`

@export If I was partly to blame for my injury or loss, does the contributory negligence rule apply to my claim (Law Reform (Miscellaneous Provisions) Act 1956 (NT) s 15, s 16, s 17(2), s 18, s 21, s 21A)? Your claim is not defeated, but your damages are reduced for your share of responsibility; the same reduction applies to a dependant's claim for a death, and to a person who suffers loss through the injured person.
GIVEN `who` IS A `Who is claiming` @desc Who is claiming
      `own failure` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the damage was partly the result of the injured person's own failure to take reasonable care
      `the act` IS A `The other person's act` @desc What the other person (or a person they are vicariously responsible for) did that partly caused the damage
      `wrong on` IS A DATE @desc The day the other person's wrong occurred
      `navigation act claim` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the claim is one to which section 259 of the Navigation Act 1912 (Cth) applies (a shipping collision claim)
      `begun before 1 August 2001` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, before 1 August 2001, legal proceedings about the wrong had been commenced or the persons responsible had agreed to settle claims arising from it
      `contract defence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the other person has a defence arising under a contract that would defeat the claim
GIVETH A `Does apportionment for contributory negligence apply to my claim`
`s 16 -- does apportionment for contributory negligence apply to my claim` `who` `own failure` `the act` `wrong on` `navigation act claim` `begun before 1 August 2001` `contract defence` MEANS
    IF `navigation act claim`
    THEN `no: s 16 does not apply to a claim under s 259 of the Navigation Act 1912 (Cth) (s 21(a), the text's no)`
    ELSE IF `wrong on` `falls before` `the commencement of the Ordinance`
         THEN `no: s 16 does not apply where the wrong occurred before 28 June 1956 (s 21(b), the text's no)`
         ELSE IF (`wrong on` `falls before` `the commencement of the 2001 amendments`) AND `begun before 1 August 2001`
              THEN `the earlier s 16 applies: the wrong was before 1 August 2001 and proceedings were commenced, or a settlement agreed, before that day (s 21A(3)); that earlier text is not encoded here (NOTHING-kind)`
              ELSE IF NOT (`own failure` AND `a wrong within s 15` `the act`)
                   THEN `s 16 does not apply: the damage was not partly the result of your own failure to take reasonable care and partly of another person's wrong (negligence, a concurrent contractual duty of care, or breach of statutory duty) (the text's no: its "if" fails)`
                   ELSE IF `contract defence`
                        THEN `s 16 does not save the claim from a defence arising under a contract (s 16(2), the text's no)`
                        ELSE `yes: your claim is not defeated by your own share of fault, but your damages are reduced as the court thinks just and equitable (s 16(1))`

-- s 16(1)(b): the damages "are to be reduced to the extent the court thinks just and equitable having regard to the
-- claimant's share in the responsibility for the damage". s 16(3): the court finds the total damages apart from any
-- limitation of liability or of jurisdiction. s 16(4): the total is reduced; if the reduced amount exceeds the limit
-- in the contract or enactment, or of the court's jurisdiction, the court awards that limit. s 16(2A): never more than
-- the maximum under a contract or enactment limiting liability. The reduction is the court's judgment, asked as a
-- percentage. Where s 16 does not apply, the answer is NOTHING: s 16 then fixes no amount.
@export How much can I recover after the reduction for my own share of fault (Law Reform (Miscellaneous Provisions) Act 1956 (NT) s 16(1)(b), (2A), (3), (4), s 17(2), s 18)? The total damages are reduced by the share the court thinks just and equitable, and the result is capped at any limit set by a contract, an enactment or the court's jurisdiction. No amount where s 16 does not apply.
GIVEN `who` IS A `Who is claiming` @desc Who is claiming
      `own failure` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the damage was partly the result of the injured person's own failure to take reasonable care
      `the act` IS A `The other person's act` @desc What the other person (or a person they are vicariously responsible for) did that partly caused the damage
      `wrong on` IS A DATE @desc The day the other person's wrong occurred
      `navigation act claim` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the claim is one to which section 259 of the Navigation Act 1912 (Cth) applies
      `begun before 1 August 2001` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, before 1 August 2001, legal proceedings about the wrong had been commenced or the persons responsible had agreed to settle
      `contract defence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the other person has a defence arising under a contract that would defeat the claim
      `total damages` IS A NUMBER @desc The total damages (in dollars) the court finds would have been recoverable but for the injured person's share of responsibility, apart from any limit
      `reduction percent` IS A NUMBER @desc The reduction (as a percentage, 0 to 100) the court thinks just and equitable for the injured person's share in the responsibility
      `a limit applies` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a contract or enactment limits the liability, or the court's jurisdiction is limited
      `the limit` IS A NUMBER @desc The maximum (in dollars) under that contract or enactment, or the court's jurisdictional limit (if none, any number; it is not used)
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`s 16 -- how much can I recover after the reduction` `who` `own failure` `the act` `wrong on` `navigation act claim` `begun before 1 August 2001` `contract defence` `total damages` `reduction percent` `a limit applies` `the limit` MEANS
    IF `s 16 -- does apportionment for contributory negligence apply to my claim` `who` `own failure` `the act` `wrong on` `navigation act claim` `begun before 1 August 2001` `contract defence`
        EQUALS `yes: your claim is not defeated by your own share of fault, but your damages are reduced as the court thinks just and equitable (s 16(1))`
    THEN IF `a limit applies` AND (`the reduced amount` GREATER THAN `the limit`)
         THEN JUST `the limit`
         ELSE JUST `the reduced amount`
    ELSE NOTHING
    WHERE
        `the reduced amount` MEANS (`total damages` TIMES (100 MINUS `reduction percent`)) DIVIDED BY 100

§§ `Part VI -- Common employment (s 22, s 22A)`

-- s 22(1): where injury or damage is suffered by a servant by the wrongful act, neglect or default of a fellow servant,
-- the employer is liable as if they had not been in common employment. s 22(2): this applies to a wrongful act, neglect
-- or default committed after the commencement of the Ordinance. "Where" failing: the text's no; before commencement:
-- the text's own limit, s 22 says nothing (NOTHING-kind).
DECLARE `Can I hold my employer liable for a fellow worker's wrong` IS ONE OF
    `yes: your employer is liable as if you and the fellow worker were not in common employment (s 22(1)); the "common employment" defence is gone`
    `s 22 does not arise: the injury was not caused by the wrongful act, neglect or default of a fellow worker of the same employer (the text's no: its "where" fails)`
    `s 22 does not reach it: the wrongful act was before 28 June 1956 (s 22(2), NOTHING-kind)`

@export Can I hold my employer liable for an injury a fellow worker caused me (Law Reform (Miscellaneous Provisions) Act 1956 (NT) s 22)? Yes: the old rule of "common employment" no longer protects the employer, and the employer is liable as for any other wrong in the course of employment.
GIVEN `fellow worker caused it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your injury or damage was caused by the wrongful act, neglect or default of a fellow worker employed by the same employer
      `wrong on` IS A DATE @desc The day of the wrongful act, neglect or default
GIVETH A `Can I hold my employer liable for a fellow worker's wrong`
`s 22 -- can I hold my employer liable for a fellow worker's wrong` `fellow worker caused it` `wrong on` MEANS
    IF NOT `fellow worker caused it`
    THEN `s 22 does not arise: the injury was not caused by the wrongful act, neglect or default of a fellow worker of the same employer (the text's no: its "where" fails)`
    ELSE IF `wrong on` `is on or after` `the commencement of the Ordinance`
         THEN `yes: your employer is liable as if you and the fellow worker were not in common employment (s 22(1)); the "common employment" defence is gone`
         ELSE `s 22 does not reach it: the wrongful act was before 28 June 1956 (s 22(2), NOTHING-kind)`

-- s 22A(1): notwithstanding any other law or contract, where an employee commits a tort for which the employer is
-- vicariously liable: (a) the employee shall not be liable to indemnify the employer; and (b) unless the employee is
-- otherwise entitled to indemnity, the employer shall be liable to indemnify the employee. s 22A(2): where the employee
-- is entitled to indemnity under insurance or a contract of indemnity, the employer is subrogated to those rights.
-- s 22A(3): subsection (1) does not apply where the employee commits serious and wilful, or gross, misconduct in the
-- course of employment and it constitutes the tort. Whether misconduct is "serious and wilful" or "gross" is a
-- judgment, asked as a fact. The "unless" in (1)(b) once met: the employer need not indemnify (fork F10).
DECLARE `Who bears the liability for my tort at work` IS ONE OF
    `you need not indemnify your employer, and your employer must indemnify you for your liability (s 22A(1)(a), (b))`
    `you need not indemnify your employer (s 22A(1)(a)); your employer need not indemnify you, because you are otherwise entitled to indemnity (s 22A(1)(b)); your employer takes over your rights under that insurance or indemnity (s 22A(2))`
    `s 22A(1) does not apply: serious and wilful, or gross, misconduct (s 22A(3), the text's no); the general law decides`
    `s 22A does not arise: your employer is not vicariously liable for the tort (the text's no: its "where" fails)`

@export If I commit a tort at work and my employer is vicariously liable, must I indemnify my employer, and must my employer indemnify me (Law Reform (Miscellaneous Provisions) Act 1956 (NT) s 22A)? You never have to indemnify your employer, and your employer must indemnify you unless you have other indemnity (such as insurance), except for serious and wilful, or gross, misconduct.
GIVEN `employer vicariously liable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your employer is vicariously liable for the tort you committed
      `serious misconduct` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the tort was serious and wilful, or gross, misconduct in the course of your employment
      `otherwise indemnified` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are otherwise entitled to indemnity for your liability (for example under an insurance policy or a contract of indemnity)
GIVETH A `Who bears the liability for my tort at work`
`s 22A -- who bears the liability for my tort at work` `employer vicariously liable` `serious misconduct` `otherwise indemnified` MEANS
    IF NOT `employer vicariously liable`
    THEN `s 22A does not arise: your employer is not vicariously liable for the tort (the text's no: its "where" fails)`
    ELSE IF `serious misconduct`
         THEN `s 22A(1) does not apply: serious and wilful, or gross, misconduct (s 22A(3), the text's no); the general law decides`
         ELSE IF `otherwise indemnified`
              THEN `you need not indemnify your employer (s 22A(1)(a)); your employer need not indemnify you, because you are otherwise entitled to indemnity (s 22A(1)(b)); your employer takes over your rights under that insurance or indemnity (s 22A(2))`
              ELSE `you need not indemnify your employer, and your employer must indemnify you for your liability (s 22A(1)(a), (b))`

§§ `Part VII -- Injury from mental or nervous shock (s 23, s 24, s 25)`

-- s 23: "member of the family, in relation to a person, means the spouse, de facto partner, parent, child, sibling or
-- half-sibling of that person"; "parent" means the father, mother, grandfather, grandmother, step-father or step-mother,
-- or a person standing in loco parentis; "child" means the son, daughter, grandson, granddaughter, step-son or
-- step-daughter, or a person to whom that person stands in loco parentis. Means definitions: a closed list.
-- s 24(1): for injury caused after the commencement, the plaintiff is not debarred from damages merely because the
-- injury arose wholly or in part from mental or nervous shock.
-- s 25(1): the liability of a person for injury caused after the commencement by act, neglect or default by which
-- another person is killed, injured or put in peril extends to injury from mental or nervous shock sustained by
--   (a) a parent or the spouse or de facto partner of that person; or
--   (b) another member of the family, where the person was killed, injured or put in peril within the sight or hearing
--       of that member.
-- s 25(5): an action on that liability shall be brought in the Supreme Court.
-- Outside the family, s 25 does not extend liability; the general law (with s 24) may still allow a claim (NOTHING-kind).
DECLARE `My relationship to the person killed, injured or put in peril` IS ONE OF
    `I am their parent (father, mother, grandparent, step-parent, or I stand in the place of a parent to them)`
    `I am their spouse or de facto partner`
    `I am their child (son, daughter, grandchild, stepchild, or they stand in the place of a parent to me)`
    `I am their sibling or half-sibling`
    `none of these`

DECLARE `Can I claim for nervous shock as a family member` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the wrongdoer's liability extends to your nervous shock as a parent, spouse or de facto partner, whether or not you saw or heard it (s 25(1)(a)); the action must be brought in the Supreme Court (s 25(5))`
    `yes: the wrongdoer's liability extends to your nervous shock as a member of the family who saw or heard it (s 25(1)(b)); the action must be brought in the Supreme Court (s 25(5))`
    `no, not under s 25(1)(b): it did not happen within your sight or hearing (the text's no: its "where" fails); a claim under the general law, which s 24 keeps open, is not decided here`
    `s 25 does not extend liability to you: you are not a member of the family within s 23 (NOTHING-kind); a claim under the general law, which s 24 keeps open, is not decided here`
    `s 25 does not arise: no one was killed, injured or put in peril by the act, neglect or default (NOTHING-kind)`
    `s 25 does not reach it: the injury was caused before 28 June 1956 (NOTHING-kind)`

@export Can I claim damages for mental or nervous shock because a family member was killed, injured or put in peril (Law Reform (Miscellaneous Provisions) Act 1956 (NT) s 23 to s 25)? A parent, spouse or de facto partner can, wherever they were; a child, sibling or half-sibling can if it happened within their sight or hearing. The action is brought in the Supreme Court.
GIVEN `caused on` IS A DATE @desc The day of the act, neglect or default
      `someone killed injured or imperilled` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether another person was killed, injured or put in peril by the act, neglect or default
      `relationship` IS A `My relationship to the person killed, injured or put in peril` @desc Your relationship to the person who was killed, injured or put in peril
      `within sight or hearing` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether they were killed, injured or put in peril within your sight or hearing
GIVETH A `Can I claim for nervous shock as a family member`
`s 25 -- can I claim for nervous shock as a family member` `caused on` `someone killed injured or imperilled` `relationship` `within sight or hearing` MEANS
    IF `caused on` `falls before` `the commencement of the Ordinance`
    THEN `s 25 does not reach it: the injury was caused before 28 June 1956 (NOTHING-kind)`
    ELSE IF NOT `someone killed injured or imperilled`
         THEN `s 25 does not arise: no one was killed, injured or put in peril by the act, neglect or default (NOTHING-kind)`
         ELSE IF (`relationship` EQUALS `I am their parent (father, mother, grandparent, step-parent, or I stand in the place of a parent to them)`) OR (`relationship` EQUALS `I am their spouse or de facto partner`)
              THEN `yes: the wrongdoer's liability extends to your nervous shock as a parent, spouse or de facto partner, whether or not you saw or heard it (s 25(1)(a)); the action must be brought in the Supreme Court (s 25(5))`
              ELSE IF `relationship` EQUALS `none of these`
                   THEN `s 25 does not extend liability to you: you are not a member of the family within s 23 (NOTHING-kind); a claim under the general law, which s 24 keeps open, is not decided here`
                   ELSE IF `within sight or hearing`
                        THEN `yes: the wrongdoer's liability extends to your nervous shock as a member of the family who saw or heard it (s 25(1)(b)); the action must be brought in the Supreme Court (s 25(5))`
                        ELSE `no, not under s 25(1)(b): it did not happen within your sight or hearing (the text's no: its "where" fails); a claim under the general law, which s 24 keeps open, is not decided here`

§§ `Part VIII -- Charge on insurance moneys (s 26, s 27, s 28)`

-- s 26(1): if the insured has a contract of insurance indemnifying them against liability to pay damages or compensation,
-- the amount of their liability is, from the event giving rise to the claim, a charge on all insurance moneys payable for
-- that liability. s 26(2): it applies notwithstanding the winding up of an insured corporation (being wound up at the
-- event, or deemed to have begun winding up no later). s 27(1): the charge is enforceable by an action against the
-- insurer, as if against the insured. s 27(3): except where s 26(2) applies, no such action may be commenced without the
-- court's leave, and leave shall not be granted where the court is satisfied the insurer may disclaim liability under
-- the contract and any proceedings needed to establish that have been taken. s 27(4): the action may be brought though
-- judgment has been recovered against the insured. The "if" failing: the text's no.
DECLARE `Can I sue the wrongdoer's insurer directly` IS ONE OF
    `yes, without leave: the insured company was being wound up (s 26(2), s 27(1), (3))`
    `yes, with the court's leave, which the court may grant (s 27(1), (3)); a judgment already obtained against the wrongdoer is no bar (s 27(4))`
    `no: leave must be refused, because the court is satisfied the insurer may disclaim liability under the policy and the proceedings to establish that have been taken (s 27(3), the text's no)`
    `Part VIII does not arise: the wrongdoer had no insurance against this liability (the text's no: the "if" of s 26(1) fails)`

@export Can I sue the wrongdoer's liability insurer directly for my damages (Law Reform (Miscellaneous Provisions) Act 1956 (NT) s 26 and s 27)? The wrongdoer's liability to you is a charge on the insurance money, which you can enforce by suing the insurer, with the court's leave unless the insured company is being wound up.
GIVEN `insured` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the wrongdoer had a contract of insurance indemnifying them against liability to pay the damages or compensation you claim
      `company being wound up` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the wrongdoer is a company that was being wound up when the event happened (or whose later winding up is treated as having begun no later than the event)
      `insurer may disclaim` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court is satisfied that the insurer is entitled under the policy to disclaim liability, and that any proceedings (including arbitration) needed to establish that have been taken
GIVETH A `Can I sue the wrongdoer's insurer directly`
`s 27 -- can I sue the wrongdoer's insurer directly` `insured` `company being wound up` `insurer may disclaim` MEANS
    IF NOT `insured`
    THEN `Part VIII does not arise: the wrongdoer had no insurance against this liability (the text's no: the "if" of s 26(1) fails)`
    ELSE IF `company being wound up`
         THEN `yes, without leave: the insured company was being wound up (s 26(2), s 27(1), (3))`
         ELSE IF `insurer may disclaim`
              THEN `no: leave must be refused, because the court is satisfied the insurer may disclaim liability under the policy and the proceedings to establish that have been taken (s 27(3), the text's no)`
              ELSE `yes, with the court's leave, which the court may grant (s 27(1), (3)); a judgment already obtained against the wrongdoer is no bar (s 27(4))`

-- s 28(1): a payment by the insurer under the contract made without actual notice of the charge is, to the extent of
-- the payment, a valid discharge to the insurer. s 28(2): the insurer is not liable under Part VIII for more than the
-- sum fixed by the contract. The charge is for "the amount of his liability". Read together: the most the claimant can
-- recover from the insurer is the wrongdoer's liability, capped at the policy sum less what the insurer has validly
-- paid out without notice of the charge (fork F11). Ranking with other claimants' charges (s 26(3)) is out.
@export What is the most I can recover from the wrongdoer's insurer under the charge on the insurance money (Law Reform (Miscellaneous Provisions) Act 1956 (NT) s 26(1), s 28)? The wrongdoer's liability to you, but never more than the sum fixed by the policy, less what the insurer paid out under the policy without notice of your charge.
GIVEN `the liability` IS A NUMBER @desc The amount (in dollars) of the wrongdoer's liability to you
      `policy sum` IS A NUMBER @desc The sum (in dollars) fixed by the policy as the most the insurer pays for this liability
      `paid without notice` IS A NUMBER @desc What the insurer has already paid under the policy (in dollars) without actual notice of your charge (0 if nothing)
GIVETH A NUMBER
`s 28 -- the most I can recover from the insurer` `the liability` `policy sum` `paid without notice` MEANS
    IF `the liability` GREATER THAN `what is left`
    THEN `what is left`
    ELSE `the liability`
    WHERE
        `what is left` MEANS IF `paid without notice` GREATER THAN `policy sum` THEN 0 ELSE `policy sum` MINUS `paid without notice`

§§ `Part VIIIA -- Damage to property caused by children (s 29A)`

-- s 29A(1): child means a person who has not attained the age of 18 years.
-- s 29A(2): where, after the commencement of the section, a child intentionally causes damage to property, a parent is
-- jointly and severally liable with the child where, when the damage was caused, the child was (a) ordinarily resident
-- with that parent and (b) not in full time employment.
-- s 29A(3): where a detainee within the meaning of the Youth Justice Act 2005 intentionally causes damage to property,
-- the Territory is jointly and severally liable with the detainee.
-- s 29A(4): the most that may be recovered from the parent or parents (sued individually or jointly) or the Territory is
-- $5,000 for the damage caused by the child or detainee.
-- s 29A(5)(b): no liability on a parent under (2) for damage caused by a detainee under (3).
-- The child remains liable without limit (s 29A(5)(a)); that is the general law, not this section.
`the s 29A cap` MEANS 5000

DECLARE `Who else is liable for the damage a child caused my property` IS ONE OF
    `the Territory, jointly and severally with the detainee, up to $5,000 (s 29A(3), (4))`
    `the parent, jointly and severally with the child, up to $5,000 from the parents together (s 29A(2), (4))`
    `no parent is liable under s 29A: the child was not living with that parent, or was in full time employment (the text's no: its "where" fails)`
    `no one else is liable under s 29A: the person was not under 18, or the damage was not intentional, or it was caused before 1 January 1992 (the text's no: its "where" fails)`

GIVEN `damaged on` IS A DATE, `under 18` IS A BOOLEAN, `intentional` IS A BOOLEAN, `a detainee` IS A BOOLEAN, `lives with the parent` IS A BOOLEAN, `full time employed` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Who else is liable for the damage a child caused my property`
`the s 29A answer` `damaged on` `under 18` `intentional` `a detainee` `lives with the parent` `full time employed` MEANS
    IF NOT (`intentional` AND (`damaged on` `is on or after` `the commencement of s 29A`))
    THEN `no one else is liable under s 29A: the person was not under 18, or the damage was not intentional, or it was caused before 1 January 1992 (the text's no: its "where" fails)`
    ELSE IF `a detainee`
         THEN `the Territory, jointly and severally with the detainee, up to $5,000 (s 29A(3), (4))`
         ELSE IF NOT `under 18`
              THEN `no one else is liable under s 29A: the person was not under 18, or the damage was not intentional, or it was caused before 1 January 1992 (the text's no: its "where" fails)`
              ELSE IF `lives with the parent` AND (NOT `full time employed`)
                   THEN `the parent, jointly and severally with the child, up to $5,000 from the parents together (s 29A(2), (4))`
                   ELSE `no parent is liable under s 29A: the child was not living with that parent, or was in full time employment (the text's no: its "where" fails)`

@export A child (under 18) or a youth detainee intentionally damaged my property: can I also recover from the child's parent or from the Territory (Law Reform (Miscellaneous Provisions) Act 1956 (NT) s 29A)? A parent the child lived with, if the child was not in full time employment, is liable with the child; for a detainee, the Territory is. Either way, at most $5,000.
GIVEN `damaged on` IS A DATE @desc The day the damage was caused
      `under 18` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person who caused the damage was under 18 at the time
      `intentional` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether they caused the damage intentionally
      `a detainee` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether they were a detainee within the meaning of the Youth Justice Act 2005 (held in youth detention) when they caused the damage
      `lives with the parent` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, at the time, the child was ordinarily resident with the parent you want to recover from
      `full time employed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, at the time, the child was in full time employment
GIVETH A `Who else is liable for the damage a child caused my property`
`s 29A -- who else is liable for the damage a child caused my property` `damaged on` `under 18` `intentional` `a detainee` `lives with the parent` `full time employed` MEANS
    `the s 29A answer` `damaged on` `under 18` `intentional` `a detainee` `lives with the parent` `full time employed`

@export What is the most I can recover from the parents or the Territory for property damage a child or youth detainee caused (Law Reform (Miscellaneous Provisions) Act 1956 (NT) s 29A(4))? The damage, up to $5,000; 0 where s 29A makes no parent or the Territory liable. The child or detainee remains liable for the rest.
GIVEN `damaged on` IS A DATE @desc The day the damage was caused
      `under 18` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person who caused the damage was under 18 at the time
      `intentional` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether they caused the damage intentionally
      `a detainee` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether they were a detainee within the meaning of the Youth Justice Act 2005 when they caused the damage
      `lives with the parent` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, at the time, the child was ordinarily resident with the parent
      `full time employed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, at the time, the child was in full time employment
      `the damage` IS A NUMBER @desc The amount (in dollars) of the damage to your property
GIVETH A NUMBER
`s 29A -- the most I can recover from the parents or the Territory` `damaged on` `under 18` `intentional` `a detainee` `lives with the parent` `full time employed` `the damage` MEANS
    IF (`the answer` EQUALS `the Territory, jointly and severally with the detainee, up to $5,000 (s 29A(3), (4))`)
        OR (`the answer` EQUALS `the parent, jointly and severally with the child, up to $5,000 from the parents together (s 29A(2), (4))`)
    THEN IF `the damage` GREATER THAN `the s 29A cap` THEN `the s 29A cap` ELSE `the damage`
    ELSE 0
    WHERE
        `the answer` MEANS `the s 29A answer` `damaged on` `under 18` `intentional` `a detainee` `lives with the parent` `full time employed`

§§ `Part X -- Dogs (s 31 to s 35)`

-- s 31: "dog means an animal of the genus Canis" (dingoes and wolves included). s 32(1): "The owner of a dog is liable
-- for any loss, damage or injury as a result of the actions of the dog." s 32(2): no need to prove a previous
-- mischievous propensity or the owner's neglect. Outside s 32 (not a dog, or the loss not from the dog's actions), s 32
-- says nothing: NOTHING.
@export A dog hurt me or caused me loss: is its owner liable (Law Reform (Miscellaneous Provisions) Act 1956 (NT) s 31, s 32)? Yes, for any loss, damage or injury resulting from the dog's actions, without proving the dog was known to be dangerous or that the owner was careless. NOTHING where s 32 does not speak (not a dog, or the loss did not come from its actions).
GIVEN `genus Canis` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the animal is a dog: any animal of the genus Canis (including a dingo)
      `from the dog's actions` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your loss, damage or injury was a result of the actions of the dog
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 32 -- is the dog's owner liable` `genus Canis` `from the dog's actions` MEANS
    IF `genus Canis` AND `from the dog's actions`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING

-- s 33(1): "It is a defence to a prosecution for or civil action in relation to the killing or injuring of a dog if:
-- (a) the defendant was not a trespasser on the premises on which the dog was; (b) the defendant or another person, or a
-- bird or animal belonging to the defendant, was, at the time that the dog was killed or injured, being attacked by the
-- dog, or the defendant believed, on reasonable grounds, that such an attack was about to take place; and (c) the
-- defendant did not provoke the attack or impending attack." s 33(2): a bird or animal belongs to the defendant if its
-- owner authorised the defendant to control it. s 33(3): a person who kills or injures a dog on premises they occupy, or
-- on which the occupier authorised them to be, is deemed to have established a prima facie defence under (1).
-- s 34(1): a person who destroys a dog under a law of the Territory, or because it is so diseased or injured that it is
-- humane to do so, is not liable. s 34(2): a person who destroys a dog at the request of another is not liable if they
-- had reasonable cause to believe the requester was the owner.
-- "It is a defence ... if" not met: ruling (b) of 2026-11-05 on defences: "this section gives no defence on these facts".
DECLARE `Do I have a defence for killing or injuring a dog` IS ONE OF
    `not liable: you destroyed the dog under a law of the Territory, or because it was so diseased or injured that it was humane (s 34(1))`
    `not liable: you destroyed the dog at the request of someone you had reasonable cause to believe was its owner (s 34(2))`
    `a defence: you were not trespassing, there was an attack (or reasonable belief in one about to happen) on you, another person or an animal or bird of yours, and you did not provoke it (s 33(1))`
    `a prima facie defence: you were the occupier of the premises, or there with the occupier's authority (s 33(3)); it can be displaced by showing that the s 33(1) conditions were not met`
    `these sections give no defence on these facts (s 33, s 34, the text's no); a defence under the general law is not decided here`

@export I killed or injured a dog: do I have a defence to a prosecution or a claim for damages (Law Reform (Miscellaneous Provisions) Act 1956 (NT) s 33, s 34)? You do if you were not trespassing, the dog was attacking (or you reasonably believed it was about to attack) you, another person, or an animal or bird of yours, and you did not provoke it; an occupier has a prima facie defence; destroying a dog lawfully, humanely or at the apparent owner's request carries no liability.
GIVEN `destroyed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you killed (destroyed) the dog, rather than injured it
      `under a law or humane` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you destroyed it under a law of the Territory, or because it was so diseased or injured that it was humane to do so
      `at the apparent owner's request` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you destroyed it at the request of someone you had reasonable cause to believe was its owner
      `not a trespasser` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were lawfully on the premises where the dog was (not a trespasser)
      `occupier or authorised` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were the occupier of the premises, or on them with the occupier's authority
      `attack` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, at the time, the dog was attacking you, another person, or a bird or animal that belonged to you or that its owner authorised you to control, or you believed on reasonable grounds that such an attack was about to happen
      `provoked` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you provoked the attack or the impending attack
GIVETH A `Do I have a defence for killing or injuring a dog`
`s 33 and s 34 -- do I have a defence for killing or injuring a dog` `destroyed` `under a law or humane` `at the apparent owner's request` `not a trespasser` `occupier or authorised` `attack` `provoked` MEANS
    IF `destroyed` AND `under a law or humane`
    THEN `not liable: you destroyed the dog under a law of the Territory, or because it was so diseased or injured that it was humane (s 34(1))`
    ELSE IF `destroyed` AND `at the apparent owner's request`
         THEN `not liable: you destroyed the dog at the request of someone you had reasonable cause to believe was its owner (s 34(2))`
         ELSE IF (`not a trespasser` OR `occupier or authorised`) AND `attack` AND (NOT `provoked`)
              THEN `a defence: you were not trespassing, there was an attack (or reasonable belief in one about to happen) on you, another person or an animal or bird of yours, and you did not provoke it (s 33(1))`
              ELSE IF `occupier or authorised`
                   THEN `a prima facie defence: you were the occupier of the premises, or there with the occupier's authority (s 33(3)); it can be displaced by showing that the s 33(1) conditions were not met`
                   ELSE `these sections give no defence on these facts (s 33, s 34, the text's no); a defence under the general law is not decided here`

-- s 35: notwithstanding any other law, (a) a person who is wholly or partially blind or deaf is entitled to be
-- accompanied by a guide-dog or hearing dog into a building or place open to or used by the public, or a vehicle, vessel
-- or craft carrying passengers for hire or reward, and commits no offence only by taking the dog in; and (b) the
-- occupier or person in charge shall not refuse entry, or deny accommodation or service, to that person by reason only
-- that they are accompanied by the dog. Maximum penalty: 4 penalty units. The value of a penalty unit is set by the
-- Penalty Units Act 2009 (NT), not deposited: the answer gives the units.
DECLARE `Can I be refused entry with my guide dog or hearing dog` IS ONE OF
    `no: you are entitled to take the dog in, and refusing you entry, accommodation or service only because of the dog is an offence (maximum 4 penalty units) by the occupier or person in charge (s 35)`
    `s 35 does not decide it: the refusal was not only because of the dog (NOTHING-kind)`
    `s 35 does not reach it: it covers a wholly or partially blind or deaf person with a guide dog or hearing dog, in a public building or place or a passenger vehicle, vessel or craft for hire or reward (NOTHING-kind)`

@export I am blind or deaf (wholly or partly) and use a guide dog or hearing dog: can I be refused entry or service because of the dog (Law Reform (Miscellaneous Provisions) Act 1956 (NT) s 35)? No, in any building or place open to or used by the public and any passenger vehicle, vessel or aircraft for hire; refusing you only because of the dog is an offence.
GIVEN `blind or deaf` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are wholly or partially blind or deaf
      `with a guide or hearing dog` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are accompanied by a guide dog or hearing dog
      `a public place or passenger transport` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the place is a building or place open to or used by the public, or a vehicle, vessel or aircraft carrying passengers for hire or reward
      `only because of the dog` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were (or would be) refused entry, accommodation or service only because the dog was with you
GIVETH A `Can I be refused entry with my guide dog or hearing dog`
`s 35 -- can I be refused entry with my guide dog or hearing dog` `blind or deaf` `with a guide or hearing dog` `a public place or passenger transport` `only because of the dog` MEANS
    IF NOT (`blind or deaf` AND `with a guide or hearing dog` AND `a public place or passenger transport`)
    THEN `s 35 does not reach it: it covers a wholly or partially blind or deaf person with a guide dog or hearing dog, in a public building or place or a passenger vehicle, vessel or craft for hire or reward (NOTHING-kind)`
    ELSE IF `only because of the dog`
         THEN `no: you are entitled to take the dog in, and refusing you entry, accommodation or service only because of the dog is an offence (maximum 4 penalty units) by the occupier or person in charge (s 35)`
         ELSE `s 35 does not decide it: the refusal was not only because of the dog (NOTHING-kind)`
